  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_venosus.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Described by Achille Valenciennes in 1840.</li><li>Common name: Veined catfish</li><li>Synonym: Tachysurus venosus</li><li>IUCN Red List: Not Evaluated</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_venosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Indo-West Pacific: Mozambique Channel to Burma (Myanmar) and southern China to Indonesia (excluding the Philippines and Australia). Found in the Mekong delta.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_venosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>It inhabits tropical marine and brackish waters in the Indo-western Pacific region, including the Mozambique Channel, Myanmar, Indonesia and southern China. Marine; brackish; demersal; depth range 20–50 m. Tropical. It dwells at a depth range of 20 to 50 m.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_venosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Feeds mainly on invertebrates and small fishes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_venosus.htm",
      "html": "<p>It reaches a maximum total length of 30 cm, but more commonly about 19 cm TL. Etymology: the specific name venosus means veiny, referring to the vein-like latero-sensory canals. Remarks: captured mainly with bamboo traps and set bagnets.</p>"
    },
    {
